{%- comment -%}
  ee-button — the two-state animated button from Figma "Component 207".

  Our own snippet, not a Dawn one (C1). It exists because the design uses the
  same component twice with different geometry and inverted colours, and the
  popup will need it again in Phase 4.

  Accepts:
    label   {String}  required — merchant-supplied text. Nothing renders without it.
    url     {String}  optional — when present an <a> is rendered, otherwise a
                      <button>, so the control stays keyboard-reachable and the
                      merchant can still see it in the theme editor before a
                      destination has been chosen.
    variant {String}  'dark' (default, hero) | 'accent' (utility-bar CTA)
    fill    {Boolean} true → the label cell grows and centres, matching Figma's
                      fixed-width centred label frame on the utility CTA
    class   {String}  optional geometry class supplied by the calling section

  Usage:
    {%- render 'ee-button',
          label: section.settings.button_label,
          url: section.settings.button_url,
          variant: 'dark',
          class: 'ee-banner__button' -%}
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if label != blank -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign ee_btn_variant = variant | default: 'dark'
    assign ee_btn_class = 'ee-btn ee-btn--' | append: ee_btn_variant
    if fill
      assign ee_btn_class = ee_btn_class | append: ' ee-btn--fill'
    endif
    if class != blank
      assign ee_btn_class = ee_btn_class | append: ' ' | append: class
    endif
  -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Captured once so the <a> and <button> branches cannot drift apart.
    The arrow is an inline SVG stroked with currentColor, which is what makes
    the colour flip carry the arrow as well as the label (DESIGN-TOKENS §3.5).

    Three things about this SVG are load-bearing, because four callers share it
    and each one gives it a DIFFERENT box:

      caller                     Figma node               drawn width
      utility-bar CTA            I1:1605;358:2736         24
      hero CTA (desktop)         3:1206 internals         25.49
      hero CTA (mobile)          3:1673                   24
      popup ADD TO CART          43:379                   33.699

    1. The path spans the viewBox EXACTLY (x 0 → 26). It used to stop at 24 of
       26, which left 2 units of dead space no caller could see or use.
    2. `preserveAspectRatio="none"`. Every one of those Figma nodes is a "Line
       15" — a horizontal rule with a height of ~0 — so the only dimension the
       design specifies is the WIDTH, and each caller sets it in CSS. The
       default `xMidYMid meet` ignores that width whenever the box is wider than
       12:26, which is why the popup's 33.7px box was drawing a 26px arrow
       centred inside it. With `none` the ink is exactly the CSS box.
    3. `vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"` is the necessary companion: without
       it, `none` would scale the stroke non-uniformly with the box, and
       `strokeWeight` is 1.5 on every one of the four nodes above.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture ee_btn_inner -%}
    <span class="ee-btn__label">{{ label | escape }}</span>
    <svg class="ee-btn__icon" viewBox="0 0 26 12" fill="none" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path d="M0 6h26M18 1l8 5-8 5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
    </svg>
  {%- endcapture -%}

  {%- if url != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `| escape` on the href: a `url` schema setting is constrained by Shopify,
      so this is not an injection route — but every other interpolation in these
      files escapes, and an unescaped attribute is not a habit worth keeping.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a class="{{ ee_btn_class }}" href="{{ url | escape }}">{{ ee_btn_inner }}</a>
  {%- else -%}
    <button class="{{ ee_btn_class }}" type="button">{{ ee_btn_inner }}</button>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
